<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elateia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Elatea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elateia was the largest city of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocis">Phocis</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, in the Cephissus valley, commanding the route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>. Its sudden occupation by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> in 339 BCE caused panic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, famously described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes">Demosthenes</a>, and led to the Battle of Chaeronea. Its ruins lie near the modern village of Elateia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elateia.jpg" alt="Elateia" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pitichinaccio · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DE_Elatias.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
